## Dynamic Theming

The theme can be changed at runtime using the `useTheme` hook:

```tsx
;

function ThemeSwitcher() {
  const { changeTheme } = useTheme();

  const setRedTheme = () => {
    changeTheme({ sourceColor: 0xFF0000 });
  };

  const setImageTheme = () => {
    changeTheme({ sourceImage: "/images/ColorExample.png" });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      Red Theme
      Image Theme
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Accessing Theme Values

You can access the current theme values using the `useTheme` hook:

```tsx
;

function ThemeInfo() {
  const { sourceColor, variant, contrast, schemes } = useTheme();

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Current source color: {sourceColor.toString(16)}</p>
      <p>Current variant: {variant}</p>
      <p>Current contrast level: {contrast}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## CSS Variables

React Material automatically generates CSS variables for all theme colors. These variables follow the naming pattern `--m3-scheme-{color-name}` and contain RGB values (without the rgb() wrapper) that you can use in your CSS:

```css
.my-custom-element {
  background-color: rgb(var(--m3-scheme-primary));
  color: rgb(var(--m3-scheme-on-primary));
}
```

Available color variables include:

- Primary colors: `primary`, `on-primary`, `primary-container`, `on-primary-container`
- Secondary colors: `secondary`, `on-secondary`, `secondary-container`, `on-secondary-container`
- Tertiary colors: `tertiary`, `on-tertiary`, `tertiary-container`, `on-tertiary-container`
- Surface colors: `surface`, `on-surface`, `surface-variant`, `on-surface-variant`
- Error colors: `error`, `on-error`, `error-container`, `on-error-container`
- And many more!

## Typography and Fonts

React Material follows Material Design 3's typography system, with **Roboto** as the default font family. The library provides built-in typography scaling and font management to ensure consistent text rendering across your application.

### Default Font (Roboto)

React Material automatically applies Roboto as the default font family for all components. The font is loaded and includes support for multiple weights and styles:

```css
/* Default font configuration in React Material */
:root {
  --m3-font: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-display: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-headline: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-title: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-label: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-body: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

The default typography scale includes:

- **Display Large**: 57px / 64px line height
- **Display Medium**: 45px / 52px line height
- **Display Small**: 36px / 44px line height
- **Headline Large**: 32px / 40px line height
- **Headline Medium**: 28px / 36px line height
- **Headline Small**: 24px / 32px line height
- **Title Large**: 22px / 28px line height
- **Title Medium**: 16px / 24px line height
- **Title Small**: 14px / 20px line height
- **Body Large**: 16px / 24px line height
- **Body Medium**: 14px / 20px line height
- **Body Small**: 12px / 16px line height
- **Label Large**: 14px / 20px line height
- **Label Medium**: 12px / 16px line height
- **Label Small**: 11px / 16px line height

### Customizing Fonts with CSS Variables

React Material provides dedicated CSS variables for different typography categories. You can override these variables to use custom fonts for specific text styles:

```css
:root {
  --m3-font: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;           /* Default font */
  --m3-font-display: 'Playfair Display', serif;         /* For display text */
  --m3-font-headline: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;   /* For headlines */
  --m3-font-title: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;      /* For titles */
  --m3-font-label: 'Roboto', system-ui, sans-serif;     /* For labels */
  --m3-font-body: 'Source Sans Pro', system-ui, sans-serif; /* For body text */
}
```

### Loading Custom Fonts

You can load custom fonts using various methods. Here are the most common approaches:

#### 1. Using @font-face

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: 'CustomFont';
  src: url('./fonts/CustomFont-Regular.woff2') format('woff2'),
       url('./fonts/CustomFont-Regular.woff') format('woff');
  font-weight: 400;
  font-style: normal;
  font-display: swap;
}

@font-face {
  font-family: 'CustomFont';
  src: url('./fonts/CustomFont-Bold.woff2') format('woff2'),
       url('./fonts/CustomFont-Bold.woff') format('woff');
  font-weight: 700;
  font-style: normal;
  font-display: swap;
}

:root {
  --m3-font-headline: 'CustomFont', system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

#### 2. Using Google Fonts

```html
<!-- In your HTML head -->
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
```

```css
:root {
  --m3-font: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-headline: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-body: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

#### 3. Using Next.js Font Optimization

```tsx
// pages/_app.tsx or app/layout.tsx
;

const inter = Inter({
  subsets: ['latin'],
  variable: '--font-inter',
});

const roboto = Roboto({
  weight: ['300', '400', '500', '700'],
  subsets: ['latin'],
  variable: '--font-roboto',
});

export default function App({ Component, pageProps }) {
  return (
    <div className={`${inter.variable} ${roboto.variable}`}>

    </div>
  );
}
```

```css
:root {
  --m3-font: var(--font-roboto), system-ui, sans-serif;
  --m3-font-headline: var(--font-inter), system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

### Font Loading Best Practices

1. **Always provide fallback fonts**: Include system font fallbacks to ensure text remains readable:

```css
:root {
  --m3-font: 'CustomFont', system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
}
```

2. **Use font-display: swap**: Ensure text remains visible during font load:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: 'CustomFont';
  src: url('./fonts/custom-font.woff2') format('woff2');
  font-display: swap; /* Shows fallback font while loading */
}
```

3. **Preload critical fonts**: Use `<link rel="preload">` for fonts used above the fold:

```html
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/CustomFont-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
```

4. **Optimize font loading**: Only load the font weights and styles you actually use.

## Best Practices

1. **Place the ThemeProvider at the root of your application** to ensure all components have access to the theme.

2. **Use semantic color names** instead of hardcoded color values in your components for better theme consistency.

3. **Test your themes in both light and dark modes** to ensure good contrast and readability.

4. **Consider accessibility** when choosing source colors and contrast levels.

5. **Provide a default theme** even if you expect users to customize it later.

6. **Use CSS variables for font customization** by overriding the `--m3-font-*` variables in your `:root` selector.

7. **Always include system font fallbacks** to ensure text remains readable even if custom fonts fail to load.

8. **Limit font weights** to only those you actually use to reduce bundle size and improve loading times.

9. **Use `font-display: swap`** in your `@font-face` declarations for better loading performance.

10. **Test typography across different devices** to ensure readability on various screen sizes and pixel densities.

